Annoying casebash :P 

Let us represent the situation with a directed graph, there is no edge between \(A\) and \(B\) if in their game they had a draw and \(A \rightarrow B\) if \(A\) beat \(B\). Then, we know that each vertex has degree \(3\) and that there are no \(3\)-cycles in the graph.

<span style="color:#f00">**<u>Claim 1:</u>**</span> For all \(n \geq 10\) such a tournament exists.

  If \(n \geq 10\), then \(u \rightarrow v\) if and only if \(v - u = \{1, 2, 3\}\), i.e. each vertex is connected with the next three vertices. Obviously, there will be no cycles in such graph of length \(3\).

<span style="color:#f00">**<u>Claim 2:</u>**</span> A tournament doesn't exist for \(n < 7\).

  However, notice that the total number of edges in the graph is exactly \(3n\), however a graph can have no more than \(\frac{n(n - 1)}{2}\) edges (because if \(u \rightarrow v\), then, \(v \not\rightarrow u\)). Thus,

  \[3n \leq \frac{n(n - 1)}{2}\]

  which is only true for \(n \geq 7\).

<span style="color:#f00">**<u>Claim 3:</u>**</span> A tournament doesn't exist for \(n = 7\) and \(n = 8\).

