Find the smallest term of the sequence  $a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots$  defined by  $a_1=2014^{2015^{2016}}$  and  $$ a_{n+1}= 
\begin{cases}
\frac{a_n}{2} & \text{ if } a_n \text{ is even} 
a_n + 7 & \text{ if } a_n \text{ is odd} 
\end{cases} $$

solution_md:
Let  $a_{n,i}$  be the number of ways to partition  $\{ 1, 2, . . . , n\}$  into  $i$  non-empty subsets. Let  $b_{n,i}$  be the number of ways to partition  $\{ 1, 2, . . . , n, n + 1 \}$  into  $i$  non-empty subsets such that consecutive numbers belong to distinct subsets. 

So  $A = \[ \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} a_{n,i} \]$  and  $B = \[ \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} b_{n+1,i} \] = \[ \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} b_{n+1,i+1} \]$  (since  $b_{n+1, 1} = 0$  for  $n \geq 1$ ).***Claim.***  $a_{n,i} = b_{n+1, i+1}$  for all  $n, i \in \mathbb{N}$ .***Proof.*** We proceed by induction on  $n$ .**Base case**:  $n = 1$ Partitions of  $\{1\}$ :  $\{\{1\}\}$  $\implies A = 1, a_{1,1} = 1.$ Partitions of  $\{1, 2\}$  such that consecutive numbers belong to distinct subsets:  $\{\{1\}, \{2\}\}$  $\implies B = 1, b_{2,2} = 1.$ **Inductive hypothesis**:  $a_{k,i} = b_{k+1, i+1}$  for all  $i \in \mathbb{N}$  and some  $k \in \mathbb{N}$ .**Inductive step**: We show that  $a_{k+1,i} = b_{k+2, i+1}$  for all  $i \in \mathbb{N}$ .

Given a partition of  $\{ 1, 2, . . . , k\}$  into  $i$  subsets, consider adding the element  $k+1$ . We may add  $k+1$  to any of the  $i$  subsets (not changing the number of subsets) or keep  $k+1$  in its own set (increasing the number of subsets by  $1$ ). Furthermore, if two partitions are distinct, they will still be distinct after adding  $k+1$ . This implies that   $$ a_{k+1, i} = ia_{k, i} + a_{k, i-1}, $$   
since there are  $i$  ways to keep the number of subsets the same and  $1$  way to add another subset.

Similarly, given a partition of  $\{ 1, 2, . . . , k, k + 1 \}$  into  $i+1$  subsets such that consecutive numbers belong to distinct subsets, consider adding the element  $k+2$ . We may add  $k+2$  to any of the  $i$  subsets which do not contain  $k+1$  or keep  $k+2$  in its own set. This implies that
\begin{align*}
b_{k+2, i+1} &= ib_{k+1, i+1} + b_{k+1, i} 
&= ia_{k, i} + a_{k, i-1} \hspace{0.8cm} \text{(by inductive hypothesis)}
&= a_{k+1, i} \hspace{2cm} \text{, as desired.} \square
\end{align*}

Thus,  $A = \[ \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} a_{n,i} \] = \[ \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} b_{n+1,i+1} \] = B$ .

