Let the square have corners  $A=(0,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D=(0,1)$ , and the laser beam be the line  $y=\frac{5}{8}x$ .
Here is the key insight:<blockquote>Instead of reflecting the laser beam, we reflect the square.</blockquote>
When the laser intersects  $\overline{BC}$ , we reflect the entire square  $ABCD$  over  $\overline{BC}$  instead of reflecting the laser over  $\overline{BC}$ .
Hence the laser is still the line  $y=\frac{5}{8}x$ , but the square is now  $A'=(2,0),B=(1,0),C=(1,1),D'=(2,1)$ .
By repeatedly reflecting the square, the trajectory of the laser will always be  $y=\frac{5}{8}x$ .

The lattice points corresponding to points  $A,B,C,D$  are  $(\text{even},\text{even}),(\text{odd},\text{even}),(\text{odd},\text{odd}),(\text{even},\text{odd})$  respectively.
As  $(8,5)$  is the first lattice point hit by the laser, then the laser hits point  $D$ , the  $\boxed{\text{upper-left}}$  corner.

